WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.000
In much of Western Europe
and America,

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.040
seasonings mean salt and pepper.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.680
They are so strong a pairing that we
even use them to describe people -

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.960
think of George Clooney and his
trademark salt and pepper hair.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.400
But where did they come from,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.880
and why do they form
such a ubiquitous pairing?

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.600
Salt is essential for life, and it’s
been used since prehistoric times,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.480
either scraped
from saltwater deposits,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.360
harvested from salty lakes,
or mined from underground.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.360
It’s one of the earliest
food industries,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.680
and one of the most important
commodities in the world.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.160
It makes food taste better, but
it's also vital for preservation -

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.600
whether of meat or vegetables.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.080
Methods for producing
and controlling it

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.280
were developed by all
the classic civilisations,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.520
most notably the Romans,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.160
who left their mark
on modern day language

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.040
with their word for salt – sal.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.200
Roman soldiers received their salary
– money to buy salt –

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.360
and salads were vegetables
and greens seasoned with salt.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.640
In the medieval period
salt was expensive,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.400
and frequently controlled
by the state.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.640
Huge, ornate vessels for the table
were made out of gold and silver,

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.880
dominating dinner tables,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.640
and indicating very clearly
who was of high status.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.800
Sitting below the salt -
i.e. not on the highest table -

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.840
well, clearly you weren’t
exactly exalted.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.080
Having salt on the table was
as much for show as practicality,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.840
for dishes were seasoned
in the kitchen,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.160
but adding salt to one’s meat –
with the tip of the knife –

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.360
was common practice,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.440
and the uncertain noble
could buy written advice

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.400
on exactly how salt etiquette worked

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.240
along with the even thornier question
of how to carve.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.200
But how did pepper come to join it?

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.000
It was one among a wide range
of spices

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.800
available to the medieval cook,
and it was expensive –

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.960
in the 1430s one pound of peppercorns
cost as much as a pig.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.400
In the late 15th Century,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.040
the Portuguese took control
of Malabar in east India,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.600
and started cultivating
black pepper on a large scale.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.680
Prices fell,
in line with over supply,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.600
and pepper became
more readily available.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.640
By the 16th Century the phrase
‘peppercorn rent’ was in use,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.040
to mean a nominal sum.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.240
The real boost though,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.080
came with a change in culinary
fashion to favour the French.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.320
They loved black pepper,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.520
and their chefs were busy tidying
and simplifying medieval cuisine,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.800
delineating more firmly
sweet from savoury,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.280
and codifying which
ingredients worked with which.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.040
Salt was obviously savoury.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.600
Pepper tended that way,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.400
and so the fashionable
French-style dishes

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.440
of the next century
used both, together,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.800
and with fewer
and fewer other spices.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.200
By the Victorian age,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.840
salt and pepper were coming together
as standard seasonings.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.560
However, other forms
of pepper vied with black,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.520
and cayenne also appeared frequently
in cookery books.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:19.920
Salt was still on the table -

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
now in small salt cellars, no longer
just for the privileged few.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.840
In 1911 the Morton Salt Company
of Chicago

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.680
patented an easy-flow salt

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.960
and salt shakers
could finally take off.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.240
During the 20th Century,
pepper shakers joined them.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.280
Then, as package holidays
introduced Brits

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.160
to freshly ground pepper
at the table,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.160
pepper mills replaced the shakers.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.040
Phew.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.600
It’s not a ubiquitous combination
everywhere though –

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.400
other cultures might
still have the salt

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.480
but replace
the black pepper with chilli.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.080
And let us not forget the pickles,
the sambals,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.480
the vinegars, the fish
sauces and the salsas.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.240
Can I make room on my table
and have them all?

